---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc954.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Patient Service Revenue — Net of Contractual Allowances and Implicit Price Concessions" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Charity Care — Never Recognised as Revenue" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Shift From \"Bad Debt Expense\" to \"Implicit Price Concessions\"" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Continuing Care Retirement Communities (CCRCs)" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Patient Service Revenue Net of Price Concessions" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — the Charity Care Exclusion" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "No Dedicated IFRS Health Care Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A multi-payor system with genuinely uncertain realizable prices</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Health care providers routinely bill patients and payors (Medicare, Medicaid, private insurers) at STANDARD gross charges that bear little resemblance to what will actually be COLLECTED — each payor negotiates or is statutorily assigned very DIFFERENT reimbursement rates. ASC 954 addresses how to recognise PATIENT SERVICE REVENUE at its genuinely EXPECTED, NET realisable amount, reflecting this multi-payor complexity, rather than at gross billed charges.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Hospitals, clinics, nursing facilities, and continuing care communities</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 954 applies to hospitals, physician practices, nursing homes, and continuing care retirement communities (CCRCs), among other health care providers.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Patient Service Revenue — Net of Contractual Allowances and Implicit Price Concessions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Recognised at the amount genuinely expected to be collected</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Patient service REVENUE is recognised NET of CONTRACTUAL ALLOWANCES (the difference between gross billed charges and the CONTRACTUALLY agreed/statutorily mandated reimbursement rate for a specific payor, e.g. Medicare's fee schedule) and IMPLICIT PRICE CONCESSIONS (amounts the provider does not genuinely EXPECT to collect from certain SELF-PAY or underinsured patients, even absent a formal contractual rate) — consistent with ASC 606's TRANSACTION PRICE principle, which requires revenue to reflect the consideration an entity expects to be ENTITLED to, not gross billed charges.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Charity Care — Never Recognised as Revenue</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">No expectation of payment from the outset</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">CHARITY CARE — services provided to patients who meet the provider's criteria for financial assistance and for whom there was NEVER an expectation of collecting payment — is <strong>NOT recognised as revenue AT ALL</strong>, even at a reduced or net amount. This differs conceptually from an implicit price concession (where SOME collection was originally expected but ultimately not realised) — charity care involves NO expectation of payment from the very outset of providing the service.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Shift From "Bad Debt Expense" to "Implicit Price Concessions"</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A genuine presentational shift under ASC 606</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">PRIOR to ASC 606, much of what health care providers historically termed "BAD DEBT EXPENSE" (amounts billed to self-pay patients that ultimately proved uncollectible) was presented as a SEPARATE OPERATING EXPENSE below revenue. Under ASC 606, that SAME economic phenomenon is now typically analysed as an <strong>IMPLICIT PRICE CONCESSION</strong> — REDUCING patient service REVENUE directly (as part of the transaction price estimate) rather than being recognised as a separate bad debt expense line — a genuine, significant PRESENTATIONAL shift, even where the underlying economic collection experience is unchanged. GENUINE bad debt expense (a downstream failure to collect an amount that WAS genuinely expected to be collected at the time of service, e.g. from an insured patient who unexpectedly fails to pay their portion) can still exist as a separate expense in more limited circumstances, but the SCOPE of what's treated this way narrowed considerably under ASC 606.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Continuing Care Retirement Communities (CCRCs)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Advance fees deferred against a future service obligation</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">CCRCs commonly collect substantial ADVANCE (entrance) FEES from residents in exchange for a LIFETIME right to occupancy and future care services. These advance fees are DEFERRED and recognised as REVENUE over the resident's remaining life expectancy (or contract term), reflecting the FUTURE SERVICE obligation the CCRC has assumed — with a corresponding "OBLIGATION TO PROVIDE FUTURE SERVICES" liability recognised if the PRESENT VALUE of expected future costs of care exceeds the present value of expected future revenues (including remaining deferred advance fees and future recurring fees).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Patient Service Revenue Net of Price Concessions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising the genuinely expected collection amount</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A hospital procedure billed across multiple payor types</div><div class="wk-body">A hospital bills $40,000 (gross charges) for a procedure. Based on the patient's insurance, the CONTRACTUALLY agreed reimbursement rate is $15,000 (a $25,000 contractual allowance), and based on historical collection patterns for the patient's remaining co-payment responsibility, the hospital estimates an ADDITIONAL $800 implicit price concession (out of a $1,200 patient co-payment portion) is unlikely to be collected.</div><div class="wk-answer">Patient service REVENUE recognised = $15,000 (contractual rate) + $1,200 (co-payment) − $800 (implicit price concession) = <strong>$15,400</strong> — reflecting the hospital's GENUINELY expected collection amount, NOT the $40,000 gross billed charge.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — the Charity Care Exclusion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">No revenue recognized at all</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A patient qualifying for the hospital's financial assistance policy</div><div class="wk-body">A patient with $22,000 of gross billed charges is determined, PRIOR to or at the time of service, to qualify for the hospital's charity care financial assistance policy, based on income and asset criteria.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the hospital NEVER expected to collect payment for this care, <strong>NO revenue is recognised</strong> for the $22,000 of charity care — this differs from an implicit price concession, where SOME collection was originally anticipated. The $22,000 is typically disclosed separately as the estimated COST (not the gross charge) of charity care provided during the period.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 954 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Estimated cost of charity care provided during the period</span><span>Disaggregation of patient service revenue by major payor source</span><span>Nature of CCRC advance fees and the obligation to provide future services</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a patient qualifies for charity care versus an implicit price concession, based on financial assistance policy criteria</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Implicit price concessions, based on historical collection experience by payor category</span><span>Present value of future CCRC service obligations relative to remaining deferred revenue</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS Health Care Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">General IFRS 15 variable consideration achieves a broadly similar net revenue outcome</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">IFRS has NO dedicated health care industry standard. A health care provider reporting under IFRS would recognise patient service revenue under GENERAL IFRS 15 principles — the SAME "transaction price" concept requiring revenue to reflect the consideration an entity EXPECTS to be entitled to (via variable consideration estimation) would produce a BROADLY similar NET revenue outcome to ASC 954's contractual allowance/implicit price concession approach, without a dedicated industry standard or the specific "charity care" terminology on the IFRS side.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 954 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Patient service revenue</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised net of contractual allowances and implicit price concessions</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Charity care</span><span>None</span><span>Not recognised as revenue; disclosed at estimated cost</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>CCRC advance fees</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Deferred, recognised over the resident's remaining life expectancy</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the general transaction price and variable consideration framework underlying patient revenue recognition</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 958</span><span>Not-for-Profit Entities — many health care entities are also not-for-profit, applying both Topics together</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Contractual allowance</div><div class="glossary-def">The difference between gross billed charges and the contractually agreed or statutorily mandated reimbursement rate for a specific payor.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Implicit price concession</div><div class="glossary-def">An amount a provider does not genuinely expect to collect from certain patients, reducing revenue directly rather than being recognised as a separate bad debt expense.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Charity care</div><div class="glossary-def">Services provided to patients meeting financial assistance criteria, for which there was never an expectation of payment, never recognised as revenue.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc954 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

